WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.040
E-learnings are extremely useful

2
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.080
They speed up your onboarding,

3
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.200
can distribute a lot of information over
a great amount of people for a low price

4
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.960
and can easily streamline trainings 
for large amounts of employees.

5
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.360
Though, they often fall short.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.640
Nobody finishes them,

7
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.720
They are not interesting 
for the target audience,

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.560
Or they merely get deployed

9
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.600
to push large amounts of information 
to your target audience

10
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.680
instead of involving
the audience.

11
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.560
That’s where we come in.

12
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.640
I’m Django van den Busken from 24Film.

13
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.800
I’m film director and producer,

14
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.960
and specialize in 
creating e-learning videos.

15
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.560
My background as a teacher,

16
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.800
my experience teaching film courses and acting classes,

17
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.400
and my knowledge of views, 
retention and clickthrough rates,

18
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.120
give me the tools necessary 
to solve these problems.

19
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.760
We work from our ‘Retention First’ Model,

20
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.280
where viewer attention is essential 
for a good e-learning.

21
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.920
In this series I want to show you 
the process of how an e-learning is created,

22
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.640
so that, by the end, you know
why our e-learnings stick around.
